<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <StartupObject>WebAPI.Program</StartupObject>
    <RootNamespace>WebAPI</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>WebAPICore</AssemblyName>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Build-time location of 3cxpscomcpp2.dll (the 3CX Call Control assembly
      this service wraps). It is a managed .NET assembly, so any OS with a
      matching .NET SDK can build against it — the path just has to be
      reachable at build time.

      Override with `dotnet build -p:PbxBinPath=/absolute/path` or by setting
      a PbxBinPath environment variable before building.

      Defaults:
        Linux (3CX v20 Debian server): /var/lib/3cxpbx/Instance1/Bin
        Windows (legacy 3CX for Windows install): C:\Program Files\3CX Phone System\Bin

      The Linux default (Instance1/Bin) is the per-instance copy laid down at
      3CX install time and NOT refreshed by PBX package upgrades — as of
      August 2026 it's still the original Sep 2022 build, predating this
      month's .NET 10 rebuild entirely. It only works as a build default
      because the compiled-against API surface has stayed stable; it does
      NOT reflect what the PBX is actually running.

      If building off-box (any Linux dev machine, not the 3CX server itself),
      scp the CURRENT copy down instead and point PbxBinPath at its folder:

        scp root@<3cx-host>:/usr/lib/3cxpbx/3cxpscomcpp2.dll /path/to/local/lib/
        dotnet build -p:PbxBinPath=/path/to/local/lib

      /usr/lib/3cxpbx/ is the system-shared copy, refreshed on every 3CX
      package upgrade — it's what the runtime AssemblyResolve hook in
      Program.cs prefers too (see the search-order comment there). Building
      against a stale Instance1/Bin reference is what let the August 2026
      3CX update (which rebuilt this DLL against .NET 10) go unnoticed at
      build time — it still compiled, then crashed at runtime with a
      FileLoadException on System.Runtime v10.0.0.0 because the deployed
      TargetFramework was still net8.0. See the parent CLAUDE.md's runtime
      debug history for the full incident.

      <Private>false</Private> keeps the DLL out of the build output — at
      runtime it is loaded from the actual 3CX install via the
      AssemblyResolve hook in Program.cs.
    -->
    <PbxBinPath Condition="'$(PbxBinPath)' == '' AND '$(OS)' == 'Windows_NT'">C:\Program Files\3CX Phone System\Bin</PbxBinPath>
    <PbxBinPath Condition="'$(PbxBinPath)' == ''">/var/lib/3cxpbx/Instance1/Bin</PbxBinPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Reference Include="3cxpscomcpp2">
      <HintPath>$(PbxBinPath)/3cxpscomcpp2.dll</HintPath>
      <Private>false</Private>
    </Reference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web implicitly references Microsoft.AspNetCore.App,
      which already includes the Configuration.*, Configuration.Binder,
      Configuration.Json, Configuration.EnvironmentVariables, Configuration.CommandLine,
      Hosting, Logging.*, and DependencyInjection packages at matching versions.
      Only list here what isn't in that framework set.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="FastEndpoints" Version="5.32.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Ini" Version="8.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
